# non-tech-user self-recovery — the protection floor's last-resort
safety net for a stuck non-technical user — apply ladder

Per `docs/plans/non-tech-user-self-recovery.md` and parent doctrine
`docs/design/loam-doctrine.md` §"Two standing constraints" #1 (the
non-negotiable floor — "always on for everyone … invisibly,
especially for a non-technical user who cannot even name [the
failures]"). This system IS that floor's safety net: when a guard
fails anyway and the user is stuck, the floor must still get them
unstuck. Owner directive Telegram 13231/13225, born from the real
6-hour silent-night comms failure.

Plan: `docs/plans/non-tech-user-self-recovery.md`.

Scope (per plan §2 + §5 + §7): EXTEND the `self-correction`
component + COMPOSE on four sealed primitives (the self-correction
user_reported trigger, dormancy detection, telegram-interface
availability+fallback, and the state-migration-engine
MigrationSafetyEnvelope snapshot/guard/restore). Four parts:

  1. Distress detection → forced self-diagnosis. A deterministic
     inbound-path hook + counter (no LLM — feedback_no_anthropic_api_key)
     trips a self-diagnosis by the 2nd distress signal (the fire-alarm
     law: 2nd signal at the latest). The diagnosis checks comms-path
     liveness + recent-actions-vs-claims — the two silent-night root
     causes. Composes the existing build_trigger_from_user_report.
  2. Watchdog. Detects a stuck/silent agent (dormancy rubric) OR a
     dead comms channel (AvailabilityProbe) proactively — no user
     signal required — and routes to recovery / outage self-heal.
  3. Plain-language recovery. The surface a non-technical user sees
     is plain-English + actionable + carries ZERO internal vocabulary
     (the abstraction-first contract; the v0.7.0 stranger-clone probe
     shape applied to the recovery surface).
  4. Safe FBM hard-reset. Resets corrupted .loam/ user-state
     BACKUP-FIRST and FAIL-CLOSED — delegating
     MigrationSafetyEnvelope.snapshot/.guard/.restore so the
     irreplaceable store is NEVER lost (a reset with no recoverable
     snapshot is REFUSED — ProtectionFloorRefusal).

AC families:

  - AC.SR-DISTRESS.1 — 2nd qualifying distress signal trips
    self-diagnosis (does not wait for an explicit "diagnose this").
  - AC.SR-DISTRESS.2 — the diagnosis checks comms-path liveness +
    recent-actions-vs-claims (the narration-not-action check).
  - AC.SR-WATCH.1 — a stuck/silent agent is detected proactively and
    routed to recovery (no user signal required).
  - AC.SR-WATCH.2 — a dead comms channel is detected and self-heal
    attempted (reconnect + out-of-band notify + queue low-confidence).
  - AC.SR-RECOVER.1 — the recovery surface is plain-language +
    actionable + satisfiable by a non-technical user.
  - AC.SR-RECOVER.2 — the recovery surface carries ZERO internal
    vocabulary (no stack traces / AC-IDs / SHAs / paths / ODD vocab).
  - AC.SR-RESET.1 — the FBM hard-reset is backup-first AND fail-closed
    (a reset with no recoverable snapshot is REFUSED).
  - AC.SR-RESET.2 — after a reset the prior .loam/ store is restorable
    byte-for-byte (the irreplaceable store is never lost).
  - AC.SR-S.1 ★ (outcome-altitude) — a real stuck/distress scenario at
    the production entry-point, NO pre-arranged state, trips detection
    → produces a real plain-language recovery (zero internal vocab) →
    for the reset branch leaves the prior store restorable
    byte-for-byte. A STUB-class test (pre-arranged trip / pre-seeded
    snapshot) does NOT satisfy it (feedback_test_outcome_altitude_required).

Method-level choices are the builder's call per ODD §1.1; the plan
fixes the four-part scope, the composition on the four named
primitives, and the AC set — not the implementation.

The LOAD-BEARING design (plan §10 F2): the highest-stakes piece is the
FBM reset (it touches the irreplaceable store). A too-eager reset is
itself the FM.DESTRUCTIVE-PRUNE betrayal applied to the user's whole
memory — so backup-first + fail-closed-refusal is a HARD invariant
(delegated to the migration-safety envelope), NOT a discipline. This
system also CLOSES named protection-matrix floor gaps: it is the first
programmatic guard over FM.NARRATION-NOT-ACTION (the silent-night
stall) and over the currently-UNNAMED FM.COMMS-PATH-DEAD (the
invisible-text bug — replies to a terminal the user never sees). FORK
F-4 decides whether this cycle also backfills those two matrix rows.

Out of scope (plan §7): a new correction/self-diagnosis engine; a new
comms transport; auto-repair of an arbitrary stuck build; the adaptive
user-model fast-down tone shift; the owner visibility window (#37).

